# Docker build context exclusions.
#
# This file is at the repository root because the build context is the
# repository root:
#
#     docker build -f docker/Dockerfile -t shc:latest .
#
# Without it the context - node_modules, virtualenvs, caches and build output -
# is copied into the daemon before a single instruction runs.

.git
.github

# Python
#
# The `**/` prefix on every rule below is load-bearing, and it was missing.
# Docker matches these patterns against each PATH COMPONENT, so a bare
# `__pycache__/` matches only `<root>/__pycache__` and NOT
# `backend/__pycache__`, `agent/tests/__pycache__` or
# `backend/alembic/versions/__pycache__`. This file already relied on that
# rule without stating it - it listed `node_modules/` AND
# `frontend/node_modules/` separately, which would be redundant if the bare
# pattern reached into subdirectories. The caches did not, so they shipped:
# `backend/.mypy_cache` alone was 48 MB of the build context, and on Windows a
# live mypy holding one of those files made `COPY backend/` fail with an
# I/O error that read like a Docker bug.
#
# `**/` fixes it at any depth, so the per-environment venv names below are no
# longer an enumeration that has to be kept in step with whatever the
# developer happens to have lying around.
**/venv/
**/.venv/
**/.venv-*/
**/env/
**/__pycache__/
**/*.py[cod]
**/.mypy_cache/
**/.pytest_cache/
**/.ruff_cache/
**/*.egg-info/
.coverage

# Node / frontend build output.
#
# frontend/node_modules and frontend/.output live inside the submodule directory
# and are named here rather than with a blanket `frontend/` rule: the
# submodule's SOURCE is needed by the frontend stage, and only its build output
# is not. Getting that backwards is the difference between a working build and a
# frontend stage that copies nothing at all.
#
# Same `**/` rule as the Python block: a bare `node_modules/` reaches only the
# repository root, which is why the nested one has always needed naming
# separately. `**/node_modules/` covers both, and any other package root that
# turns up, in one entry instead of an enumeration to maintain.
**/node_modules/
frontend/.output/
frontend/.nitro/
frontend/.tanstack/
frontend/.wrangler/
frontend/.lovable/
frontend/test-results/
frontend/playwright-report/

# Local configuration and secrets.
#
# backend/core/config.py loads env_file=<repo root>/.env, so a developer's local
# .env - which commonly holds SHARED_TOKEN and a SECRET_KEY - is readable here.
# Baking it into an image would publish the operator's credentials to anyone who
# pulls it. Variables are passed explicitly as --build-arg / -e instead.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
frontend/.env
frontend/.env.*
!frontend/.env.example

# Runtime state that must never be baked into an image. These are the operator's
# data, not source: shipping them would put a database and a JWT signing key
# into a published image, and a committed signing key has to be rotated on sight
# (issue #56).
#
# `**/` again. These three rules looked correct and did nothing, because they
# only ever matched the repository ROOT - and the databases they were written to
# keep are not at the root:
#
#     backend/scientific_home_cluster.db          139 KB, shipped in shc:latest
#     backend/scientific_home_cluster.db-shm       32 KB, live shared-memory index
#     backend/scientific_home_cluster.db-wal            0 KB, live write-ahead log
#     backend/tests/unit/scientific_home_cluster.db
#
# All four were copied into the image by `COPY backend/ ./backend/` and were
# readable by anyone who pulled it. The `-shm` and `-wal` companions are the
# give-away: those files exist only beside a database that was OPEN at build
# time, so a developer's live cluster state - nodes, jobs and their commands -
# was travelling inside the published image. Anyone who can `docker pull` can
# read it, which is the same exposure issue #56 recorded for signing keys.
**/data/
**/.shc/
**/*.db
**/*.db-shm
**/*.db-wal
**/*.sqlite
**/*.sqlite3

# Stray local artefacts that are in the working tree but not part of the
# distributed packages (setuptools includes only backend*, shared*, agent*,
# cli*; stubs/ is mypy-only).
~/
src/
stubs/
docs/
*.md
acceptance_comment.md
close_comment.md
pr_body.md
mypy_output.txt